  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Build Steps**\n\nOkay, so I\'m digging into how the build process works. I see that `npm run lint` uses `tsc --noEmit`, while `npm run build` runs `vite build`. I\'m also noting the setup in `index.html` with module scripts and linked stylesheets, aiming to understand the complete file integration. I think I\'m starting to get a good picture of the key components here.\n\n\n**Verifying Build Process**\n\nOkay, so I\'m focusing on verifying the build process, specifically how `tsc --noEmit` and `vite build` behave under different import scenarios. I\'m noting the `tsconfig.json` settings: `paths`, `noEmit`, and `allowJs`. My goal is to confirm they pass without errors when certain files are not explicitly imported, or when only JavaScript is present. I need to make sure I understand the error conditions.\n\n\n**Defining Visual Style**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of visual style. I\'m noting the color palette\342\200\224pure white, grays, and a sparing use of red for accents. I\'m focusing on the font choices: \"Inter Tight\" for headlines and \"Inter\" for body text, both sourced from Google Fonts. Also, I\'m examining logo implementation, considering local image fallback and dark mode adjustments using `filter: brightness(0) invert(1)`. I\'m also planning to place the `img/logo.png` in the project root/public. Finally, the overall aesthetic will be inspired by Apple.com, emphasizing vast spaces and minimalistic design.\n\n\n**Designing User Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on UI specifics, particularly for the preloader and hero section. I\'m planning a numerical counter animation (0-100) with a 2.5-second duration, followed by a fade-out to reveal the hero section with a staggered effect. I\'m also planning a fixed, transparent navigation bar that becomes opaque upon scrolling, with specific color and blur effects. For the hero, I\'m working with a red \"eyebrow\" text with a typewriter effect, a dynamic gradient H1, and a centered, light-gray subtitle.\n\n\n**Detailing Website Sections**\n\nI\'m now getting into the design of specific website sections. I\'m focusing on the **Inicio** section first. I\'m envisioning a hero with a red \"eyebrow\" text and a dynamic H1, along with two call-to-action buttons with magnetic effects, and a vertical animated red scroll indicator with accompanying text. Next, I\'m thinking about the **Nosotros** section, which will feature a two-column grid. The left side will have a label and heading, while the right side will present the company\'s story. Inline stats will animate on scroll. Finally, I\'ve outlined the **Servicios** section, also a 2x2 grid, with four interactive cards; each card features a brief description and an icon.\n\n\n**Elaborating Service Section**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Servicios\" section further. For \"Cobertura Nacional,\" I\'m planning a two-column grid: a visual with three concentric red rings and pulsing red dots representing key cities, and a text section with a heading and paragraph. Below, there will be four main routes listed with a vertical red line. For \"Flota,\" I\'m designing three vertical items, each a grid with a geometric visual and stylized icon paired with information (heading, paragraph, specs). On hover, these shapes will rotate and change opacity. Finally, I\'m envisioning a 4-column grid banner for key statistics with animated counters and subtle borders.\n\n\n**Structuring Contact/Footer**\n\nI\'m now putting together the Contact and Footer sections. For contact, I\'m using a two-column grid with detailed contact info on the left, and a full-width interactive form on the right. The footer will be a 4-column grid with brand info, navigation, services, and intranet links, along with a copyright bar.\n\n\n**Implementing Scroll Visuals**\n\nI\'m now implementing scroll-based visual effects using CSS `clip-path` transitions. I\'m focusing on five shapes: circles, a diamond, a hexagon, and a triangle, each with distinct sizes, positions, and gradients. These forms will transition dynamically across sections, changing size, position, and opacity based on the active section and applying a 1.2s cubic-bezier timing function.\n\n\n**Refining Animation Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing more of the animations and effects. I\'ve noted the \"ease out el\303\241stico Apple\" and am focusing on scroll reveal, animated counters, custom scrollbar, `::selection` styling, subtle parallax for geometric figures, a grain/noise overlay using an SVG filter, and a custom cursor. The Javascript only sets `data-section`, and the CSS handles the rest with data attributes.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 887327375
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
