  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Vite Build Errors**\n\nI\'ve got a specific Vite build error to report: A warning popped up about a script tag referencing `/script.js` in `index.html`. It looks like the issue is that the script tag didn\'t have the `type=\"module\"` attribute, so Vite skipped bundling it.\n\n\n**Investigating Pathing Issues**\n\nI\'ve been examining the implications of absolute paths within the exported ZIP. Specifically, when `index.html` uses `/script.js` or `/style.css`, it breaks if the user opens the file directly in their browser from the downloaded folder. The leading slash directs the browser to the root filesystem. I\'m also considering how this impacts scenarios when `npm run build` is executed, and whether placing assets in the `public/` directory resolves these issues. Using relative paths like `./` might offer a robust solution.\n\n\n**Confirming Public Directory Behavior**\n\nOkay, so I\'ve been verifying the role of the `public/` directory in a Vite build. It seems anything placed in `public/` is directly copied to the `dist/` directory. If I use relative paths like `./style.css` and `./script.js` in `index.html`, this resolves correctly whether I\'m opening the file locally, using a server, or after a build. This looks like the solution to the pathing issue.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
      nanos: 687479462
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
